<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Viroconium Cornoviorum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Viroconium, Uriconium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Viroconium Cornoviorum, at Wroxeter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was the capital of the Cornovii in Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and one of the largest Roman towns in the province. It grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of the mid-1st century CE into a civitas capital with a forum and great public baths. Unlike most Roman towns, it was rebuilt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and lived on into the 5th and 6th centuries, possibly as a seat of post-Roman rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/viroconium-cornoviorum.jpg" alt="Viroconium Cornoviorum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alastair Rae from London, United Kingdom · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viroconium_Cornoviorum_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
